13. Challenges Faced
Selecting useful network traffic features.
Preparing clean and correctly labelled data.
Training and evaluating the machine learning model.
Reducing false-positive and false-negative predictions.
Understanding the limitations of demonstration data.
14. Future Scope
Use real, appropriately licensed datasets such as CIC-IDS2017 or UNSW-NB15.
Compare Random Forest with Decision Tree and Logistic Regression.
Develop a graphical dashboard.
Add CSV upload and traffic classification.
Evaluate false-positive rates and performance on unseen traffic.
Explore real-time monitoring in an authorized test environment.       
15. Conclusion
The Machine Learning Based Intrusion Detection System demonstrates how supervised machine learning can be applied to cybersecurity. The Random Forest algorithm classifies input records and provides evaluation metrics. This project provides a foundation for understanding automated intrusion detection. Further testing with representative network traffic is necessary before the system can be considered suitable for real-world use.      
